 gone up  the gap between high performing and low  performing centers remains but the whole  curve is moved up because everybody kind  of knows how they're doing everybody  kind of knows that they better they  better revisit they better go talk to  somebody else and figure out how they're  doing it better but until we start  measuring outcomes we have lost our most  powerful tool for improving value in  healthcare and measuring cost is an  equally great a priority what's bundle  reimbursement but no reimbursement is a  total package price for the hair cycle  of a medical condition including the  responsibility for avoidable  complications so essentially if you're  getting a bundle price for knee  replacement you'll get a price that  includes the pre sort of the pre-surgery  visits the actual procedure and then all  the rehab until a certain point after  you know three months later six months  later a bundle price means that if you  have an infection then you the provider  have to bear the costs of dealing with  that infection you don't get more money  if there's an infection now there's  sometimes there's unavoidable  complications there's things that nobody  could ever predict good bundle pricing  systems have a sort of have a way of  dealing with us so that you're not  penalized for that a good bundle pricing  system is severity adjusted you know a  complex total knee with a 75 year old  gets a different bundle than a kind of a  simple company for a relatively younger  healthier patient we now have sort of  dipped our toe a little bit in bundle a  bundle bundle payment payment for short  episodes that's what a DRG is but until  we ultimately start paying for the whole  cycle of care we create a real  disconnect with with value and there are  many bundles pilots going on as we speak  there's a tremendous movement in this  direction those either medicine by the  time you really get well into your  professional career this is the way  you're going to be paid because it's the  only way that we can actually align the  everybody's incentives but it's a  complicated thing to work out and  there's lots of details to be learned  about and this is something that all  people in the healthcare field are going  to need to understand it and learn how  to do because again I think it's  inevitable it's already happening  integration of provider systems i think  is intuitively pretty clear growing  excellent providers across geography you  know here's an example from chop  Children's Hospital of Philadelphia  normally number one ranked pediatric  hospital in America chopped has a main  campus right next to you pan hospital  many of you probably know know that area  but you may not know that chop also has  a large network of other or units that  actually deliver pediatric care some of  them are inpatient units those inpatient  units tend to be inside of a community  hospital but actually chop is running  that inpatient unit it's chop positions  and chop staff that are actually  managing that inpatient unit even though  it's in a community hospital that's not  part of chopped in terms of ownership  they have a variety of and that's  represented by these hospitals these are  all inpatient units for both newborn  care and and and pediatric care that  that chops staffs even though in most of  these cases they don't actually own own  the hospital there's another there's  another set of probably a 25 or 30  outpatient units some primary care some  specialty care that are spread all over  the tri-state area that actually chop is  managing itself so to go to chop you  don't just go to the downtown hospital  you can get chopped quality in a huge  tri-state region and what's very  exciting about this model at chop and  and and and why we wrote a case on it is  that the care in all these units is  actually connected it's not that each of  these units is kind of a standalone  thing it's these units are all really  part of an integrated organization so  that if a kid needs a certain kind of  care they can get it in the in the  outline community hospital but in a  serious case there's a seamless  transition to get that child to the more  acute facility there's a there's a kind  of a sense that this is an integrated  network that's getting the patient to  the right place to get the right kind of  care as part of an integrated process  this is still very rare most hospital  systems are not hospital systems they're  just they're just collections of  independent hospitals that are often  very balkanized and then separate and  then finally information technology is a  critical piece of value-based delivery  this slide and by the way these slides  will be posted so you can you can look  at them at your leisure this slide kind  of talks about what are the  characteristics of the kind of IT  systems that we actually need in order  to enable a very high value model you  know some some organizations are getting  closer but we still have a long way to  go and probably the number one  constraint right now is standards we  don't have clear enough standards to be  sure that we're all talking the same  language that we're all defining things  exactly the same way that we're  measuring things with the same number of  significant digits and therefore the  system still are not really able to talk  and compare and aggregate data  effectively that day is coming but the  the task is is still far from completed  now let's talk a little bit about global  health and then we'll open it up so if  we go from America  or Sweden or Japan or Germany and we  start thinking about Rwanda or Brazil or  Peru you know how do we kind of take  these basic ideas and and kind of adapt  them and think about them in these  resource-poor settings and clearly a  resource-poor setting is different  there's less resources the patient is in  a fundamentally different situation than  in an advanced economy and but what is  been very surprising to me and to our  colleagues in the global health project  at Harvard it's just how many of the  same issues apply to global health as  apply to the more general questions in  advanced economies we have in global  health the tendency to think that the  product is treatment that if we provide  the arv drugs then we succeeded rather  than to understand that the problem is  health it's how well the patient  actually does so many global health  projects measure success based on volume  how many screenings how many how many  how many tests how many drug drug  prescribing occurred rather than  actually the value of the services  provided you know how well the patient  actually did we succeed if we get the  patient on ARVs but we don't really know  how weather the patient had a good  outcome because they don't even  measuring in global health just like we  don't measure it in in advanced economy  settings in global health there's a  tremendous pressure to think in terms of  interventions many NGO programs and  other programs in global health are  organized around individual  interventions rather than thinking about  really the whole care cycle for the  patient there's a tendency to kind of  look narrowly to individual diseases the  so-called kind of vertical view that we  need to think about malaria rather than  to understand that many diseases are  really interconnected and so the medical  condition is not the narrow disease it's  kind of a set of related diseases that  that tend to occur together and probably  the most debilitating thing  all is that global health is massively  fragment and the advent of NGOs and lots  of money has just made the problem worse  we have hundreds if not thousands of  standalone programs each of which is  reinventing the wheel people are  passionate they care they're working  hard but we have these little programs  that are completely disconnected with  each other and they're not part of a  system and they're not operating at  anything like the scale necessary to  actually be particularly effective so we  have a very similar set of problems  ironically despite the very different  setting involved so in this is just a  picture that Jim Kim my co-author in  this area you know found and this is  just one country and all the  stakeholders and the entities involved  in the delivery system in a country like  Cannes Ania and this doesn't even  include all the hundreds of other  organizations actually a delivering care  we're not looking at this as a delivery  system we're not looking at it in terms  of integrated care we've got to learn  how to do that so we have as we have  been doing case studies and working in  this area we've come to believe that  that that at least at the broadest level  the global health delivery systems need  to start kind of being designed and  developed with a set of fundamental  principles they really involve kind of  four basic sort of analytical levels one  is the whole notion of the care delivery  value chain for individual medical  conditions when we think about hiv/aids  or malaria or mother and child health  we've got to think not about the  interventions we got to think about the  whole delivery systems for dealing with  those diseases and as we deliver care in  resource-poor settings however we are  going to need a shared delivery  infrastructure in which we can actually  deliver care but but but but in setting  in a setting where we're going to have  to take advantage of the  efficiencies of scale and utilization  that come from sharing infrastructure  across multiple diseases so how do we  design that shared infrastructure in  order to be a good platform for the  actual care delivery itself of course in  global health a fundamental issue is  aligning the design of the delivery  system with the external contacts facing  the patient the patient's access to  transportation the patient's nutritional  status the patient's you know family and  its kind of social norms and stigmas and  so forth in global health it's even more  important that it is in advanced  economies to kind of recognize that that  the circumstances facing the patient  actually have a fundamental impact on  the actual design of the right way to  deliver care and then and this is  something I've been particularly focused  on myself you know how can we actually  as we design delivery systems in  resource-poor settings how can we design  systems that not only provide health  care but also maximally leverage  economic and social development in the  communities in which in which these  systems are operating we tended to see  the there be there to be a competition  between economic development and health  system development we tended to see  these as competing priorities for  resources I think what we're coming to  understand is actually they're extremely  synergistic and that we can leverage our  health system development to very very  good end for economic development if we  kind of understand where those leverage  points are now in terms of the first  level of thinking I think this is a very  busy chart but this is really the  calorie value chain for hiv/aids and  what this says is that in dealing with  hiv/aids we can't just think about a  screening program we can't just think  about a RV program we can't just think  about you know clinical deterioration  programs or hospice type programs we've  got to think about these things  systemically as sort of a set of  interrelated  activities and interventions and and and  this is this is a process of research  and experimentation that it is just  underway we now have about a dozen case  studies of the best a HIV programs we  can find around the world and what we're  starting to understand is that the best  programs are ones that have been able to  kind of get out of the silos and think  about how they can actually provide much  more effective and care bye-bye thinking  more across the value chain rather than  to see themselves as just doing one one  isolated and discreet step again the  time does not permit me to to kind of  delve into the details of each of these  areas but but let's just talk a little  bit about why you need to think about  the whole value chain not just about the  individual interventions you know here's  just here just for four examples now  what we know is that that if we're going  to do prevention well we can't think  about prevention as sort of a generic  task we've got to think about prevention  in the sense of who's actually at risk  you don't create value by by doing some  preventative step to somebody who's not  at risk the way you create value is you  figure out who's really at risk and  putting your resources for a convention  in a very surgical way against those  people that's kind of an example of kind  of how value thinking might affect what  we what we actually do in practice you  know early diagnosis helps not only for  its own sake but it also helps in  actually for stalling the progression of  the disease so well if we can diagnose  early we get a benefit downstream we get  a benefit because we don't have to  really start the expensive therapy until  later if we if we manage the patient  well how we do something earlier in the  process affects us later in the process  you know intensive evaluation and  treatment at the time of diagnosis can  forestall a disease profession it may be  you may seem expensive to to treat  somebody that doesn't have you know that  doesn't need drugs and we just leave  those people to wait until the time that  they need drugs but it turns out that if  do treat them we can create a lot of  value because we can really stretch out  the period before extensive drugs are  necessary and another kind of classic  example of value thinking is if we can  get compliance to be very high for the  first stage therapy we can avoid the  need to go to a much more costly second  stage therapy so we would be willing to  invest a lot in compliance because it's  going to save us a lot of money later on  in the process so things like Cuban  health workers may be a real bargain  because the community health worker who  can watch that patient actually take the  drug and make sure they have it and make  sure they comply will save us the need  to pay for a five times or ten times  more expensive course of therapy later  on again that's that's that's kind of  value thinking but when we're organized  around interventions when we're  measuring success based on volume not  value kind of we missed those critical  points now again we could talk about the  this sort of analytical mind set at all  these levels you know how we design the  shared delivery infrastructure to kind  of maximize value how do we integrate  the horizontal and vertical in a way  that that creates the best possible  outcome per dollar of cost again some  implications just taking hiv/aids up you  know what what are the relevant aspects  of external context one of those things  that are going to affect our success in  actually caring for patients in  providing a care and and what do we know  about the best possible way in which to  deal with these various factors there's  a tremendous amount of knowledge about  this in the global health field but it's  never been pulled together this is a  field of lots of little papers on lots  of independent subjects and there's been  really no way of kind of aggregating our  knowledge about all of these things and  transmitting that knowledge to  practitioners in the field who have to  figure out how to design their systems  and actually provide care in a highly  imperfect world with limited resources  so how can we kind of start  you accumulate our knowledge base about  about each of these factors and how to  best deal with them again here's some  some implications again for the case of  HIV where we've done a lot a lot of work  on that and then of course the the  impact on economic development I think  it's well understood that that healthier  people are necessary for a productive  economy I think we all understand that  if people aren't healthy they can't work  they can't be productive they can't they  can't create a benefit to the economy  but what is less understood is how the  health system itself is a driver of  economic development through the direct  employment but also through the  procurement and through the catalytic  effect that the health system can have  on the kind of critical infrastructure  necessary for enabling economic  development in poorer settings and this  is since I'm an economic development  scholar as well this bridge between the  health system and the economic  development it is something of  particular interest to me again there's  there's there's material written about  this that we can share with you now a  lot of the thinking in global health has  been on public policy what's the right  public policies that will move the  health system forward but I think we've  often gotten the cart before the horse  we really lack a shared understanding of  what a high value delivery system is in  a resource for setting what it would  look like how these elements interrelate  you know what the role of the various  clinics and hospitals and other  institutions is how to see this as truly  a system until we understand what a high  value system looked like it's pretty  hard to set public policies that are  actually going to work and drive us  towards our goals so we believe very  strongly that we start we need to add  another level to the research and  ultimately to the practice in this field  we've got a lot of work going on basic  science and that work hopefully will  continue and hopefully we'll have more  breakthroughs and more progress and you  know people like the Gates Foundation  are pumping you know billions of dollars  in into basic science to kind of improve  the tools available there's a lot of  work in healthcare on on clinical  science on you know what's the proper  diagnosis what's the proper intervention  you know how do you think about that how  do we learn how to do that better you're  really really important oh you know  there's a whole established literature  around clinical trials and and and in  other methodologies for doing this work  there's a growing body of working  evaluation science you know does this  intervention work is it cost-effective  unfortunately a lot of that work though  is is kind of is kind of not very  connected to reality because it's  controlled experiments it's holding a  lot of things constant and varying only  one thing and we know that in real life  everything's always varying and we don't  have controlled experiments and so we  need we need to learn how to deal with  the complexity of the actual delivery so  we believe that there's another body of  work necessary which is what we would  call delivery science which which which  looks at some different kinds of  questions now how our interventions best  delivered how do we look what do we know  from the many organizations in the world  that are trying to do this how do we  know which ones are most effective which  approaches are most effective a very  very not a field in that area yet you  know how can the whole set of  interventions be integrated over time  how do you put these things together how  do you really adapt delivery to specific  kinds of local circumstances what what  do we know about that and then of course  how do we measure value not just you  know how many drugs were prescribed or  or or how many patients were served but  but actually how well the patient's did  and how do we develop new techniques and  methodologies for doing that as I  mentioned before we have a project at  Harvard called the global health  delivery project we have a tremendous  body now of case studies that you might  be interested in these these are the  organizations that now have in depth on  site clinical deep  studies done of what did they do and how  is it going and and and and it which  allow us to really dig into what creates  effective health care delivery models  how do you organize how do you reward  the the workers you know how do you best  reach out and engage patients there's a  wealth of learning out there but we've  never had a mechanism for for pulling it  together so our hope is that that some  of you that are passionately interested  about global health will will will will  kind of tap into this work if you're  interested become part of it we would  love to have a bunch of people at Yale  working in this area as well we'd love  to share all we have with you because we  you know we've global health has been a  field where you know there's been a lot  of passion and and people have viewed  with with great does it have a  tremendous desire to do good here and  it's been a field where kind of let's  call it values have been very important  you know we we have an obligation to  help people in resource-poor settings  get health care but you know Jim Kim and  I in particular my co-author and much of  this work we think it's time to move  from values we care to value we're  really creating great outcomes  efficiently and and that's kind of the  place where where we think this field is  kind of needs to move in the future so  hopefully some of you will be moved to  read some of this material get in touch  with this and and we and we'll look  forward to that
 

